const LESSONS = [
{id:1,phase:'Foundations',title:'From a trade to a value',minutes:8,teaser:'Separate notional, price, market value and profit.',take:'A trade describes a contract. A valuation estimates what that contract is worth at a particular time.',body:[
'Start with a bond: the issuer borrows money, pays coupons and promises principal at maturity. The holder owns those future cash flows. A quoted price of 98.50 usually means 98.50 per 100 of face value. It does not mean the whole position is worth 98.50.',
'An equity position is different: shares × currency price per share gives market value. A swap exchanges streams of cash flows, such as fixed interest for floating interest. Its notional helps calculate payments; the notional is usually not the swap’s market value. An option gives a right, rather than an obligation, and its value depends on the possible future outcomes as well as today’s underlying price.',
'Mark-to-market uses relevant market prices. Mark-to-model calculates value from contractual cash flows and market inputs. A model is not automatically unreliable: a vanilla swap can have a well-supported model value. Equally, a price on a screen is not automatically suitable evidence for your exact position.',
'Always attach a valuation date, currency, position quantity, product identifier and convention to a number. “Value = 9.85m” without those attributes is an incomplete record.'
],example:{title:'One position, four different numbers',text:'Face amount       $10,000,000\nClean price        98.50 per 100\nClean market value $9,850,000\nAccrued interest   $40,000\nDirty value        $9,890,000',explain:'Clean price excludes accrued interest; dirty value includes it. We keep accrued interest fixed in the bond labs so a change in clean price is easy to isolate.'},deep:'A position is often an aggregation of several trades. A trade identifier and a security identifier are not interchangeable. One bond can appear in many trades and books. Equity quantity, bond face amount and derivative notional require different valuation functions. Contract features such as settlement date, coupon schedule, optionality and collateral agreement can change the cash flows or discounting.',data:'Expected fields: TradeId, InstrumentId, Product, Book, ValuationDate, Currency, SignedQuantity or SignedNotional, PriceConvention, AccruedInterest. Check identifier joins and units before investigating the pricing model.',pitfall:'A $100m swap notional is not a $100m asset. A change of 0.15 price points on a per-100 quote is 0.15% of face value, not 15%.',quiz:{q:'A $20m bond face amount is priced at 99.65 per 100. Ignoring accrued interest, its value is…',options:['$19,930,000','$99.65','$20,000,000'],correct:0,explain:'$20,000,000 × 99.65 / 100 = $19,930,000. Face amount and market value serve different purposes.'},sources:['ifrs','basel'],lab:'bond'},
{id:2,phase:'Foundations',title:'What fair value is trying to measure',minutes:12,teaser:'Understand exit value and why a mid price needs context.',take:'Fair value asks what market participants would exchange at the measurement date, rather than what you hope the position will earn.',body:[
'Imagine two separate questions: “What did we pay?” and “What is it worth today?” Historical transaction price answers the first. Fair value answers the second using an orderly market-participant exit perspective. It is not a forced fire-sale estimate or a promise of the exact price your next trade will achieve.',
'A principal market is the market with the greatest volume and activity for the instrument that the entity can access. This matters when screens show different prices on different venues. Valuation uses the relevant market and consistent measurement-time assumptions.',
'For a simple long position, the bid is what a dealer offers to pay; the ask is what they charge to sell. Mid sits between them. Accounting permits the point within the spread that best represents fair value; bid for assets and ask for liabilities are permitted conventions, not an automatic universal formula. Transaction costs are not part of IFRS 13 fair value.',
'A valuation method must produce a result consistent with the fair-value objective and supported by the contract and evidence.'
],example:{title:'A spread is a range, not two contradictory prices',text:'Dealer bid         99.80\nDealer ask        100.20\nMid               100.00\nLong face amount  $10m\nMid-to-bid cost    $20,000',explain:'The $20k illustrates the economics of exiting at bid. Whether and how a booked adjustment is required depends on the existing mark and applicable accounting policy.'},deep:'A quoted price for an identical instrument in an active market generally has priority. Holding a large block does not automatically justify an accounting blockage discount to that Level 1 quote. Prudential concentration adjustments answer a different question. Keep the unit of account, the market-participant premise and the purpose of the adjustment separate.',data:'Keep PriceSide (bid/mid/ask), CleanDirty, QuoteTime, Market and SettlementDate alongside Price. Two valid prices can look inconsistent because their conventions or timestamps differ.',pitfall:'Do not subtract bid-offer twice when the front-office value is already on the relevant exit basis. Do not turn every liquidity concern into an accounting discount.',quiz:{q:'The front office marks at bid and an independent source reports mid. What should you do first?',options:['Book the full difference immediately','Align the price conventions and timestamps','Classify the position Level 3'],correct:1,explain:'Compare like with like before deciding whether a genuine valuation difference remains.'},sources:['ifrs','ifrsdetail'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:3,phase:'Foundations',title:'Market data and the model underneath',minutes:15,teaser:'Curves, discount factors, spreads, volatility and sensitivity.',take:'A model turns a contract and a set of inputs into a value. Changing the input can change the value without changing the contract.',body:[
'A discount factor converts a future cash flow to today’s value. In a simple annual-compounding example, PV = cash flow / (1 + rate)^years. A curve is a set of rates or discount factors across maturities. Real pricing also needs calendars, day-count conventions, interpolation and settlement rules.',
'For a fixed-versus-floating swap, think “PV of what I receive minus PV of what I pay.” Forecast curves estimate future floating payments; discount curves bring the payments back to today. Collateral and currency affect the appropriate curves. A curve mismatch can therefore produce an IPV difference even when both systems use the same contract.',
'A credit spread is an additional yield associated with credit and other market effects relative to a reference. Volatility describes the variability used in option pricing; an implied volatility is backed out of traded option prices. A volatility surface varies by strike and maturity. Correlation describes joint movement and can matter greatly for multi-asset products.',
'Sensitivity connects input differences to currency amounts. DV01 is a rate sensitivity per basis point under a stated sign convention. Vega measures sensitivity to volatility under a stated unit. Exact repricing is the safer reference when nonlinear effects are material.'
],example:{title:'A small input gap can mean a large value gap',text:'Signed sensitivity  +$12,000 per bp\nIndependent − FO    −2 bp\nLinear value change −$24,000',explain:'This is a first-order estimate, with sensitivity explicitly defined as dV for a +1bp move. Some systems display DV01 using the opposite sign. Always inspect the definition.'},deep:'An input difference is not directly a price difference. For a nonlinear instrument, ΔV is approximately delta × Δx plus one-half gamma × Δx², with cross terms when several inputs move. Sensitivity-based approximations are useful for prioritising exceptions, but replacing the complete input set and repricing avoids assuming all changes are small and independent.',data:'CurveId, Tenor, QuoteUnit, CurveDate, CalibrationVersion, DiscountingAgreement and ModelVersion are part of lineage. A percent/decimal error (5 vs 0.05) can dwarf genuine market movements.',pitfall:'1 basis point = 0.01 percentage point = 0.0001 in decimal rate. One volatility point usually means 1 percentage point, but verify the system’s vega unit.',quiz:{q:'Why can two systems value an identical swap differently?',options:['The swap notional must differ','They may use different curves, conventions or valuation times','A model value can never be verified'],correct:1,explain:'Identical trade data is necessary but not sufficient. Market data, model configuration and conventions must also align.'},sources:['basel'],lab:'pv'},
{id:4,phase:'Valuation control',title:'IPV: an independent challenge',minutes:9,teaser:'Follow the full process from population to sign-off.',take:'IPV tests whether prices or model inputs are independently supportable. It is a process, not merely a subtraction.',body:[
'Independent price verification adds a control separate from the desk that owns the position. Basel guidance distinguishes it from daily marking and calls for independent verification at least monthly, with greater frequency where appropriate. Your bank can impose a more frequent cycle.',
'Begin by reconciling the population: which positions are in scope, and are any missing? Obtain independent evidence, validate the source, align units and conventions, compare or reprice, investigate differences, then record a conclusion. A control is incomplete if it produces an exception but nobody owns the resolution.',
'Direct-price IPV compares a usable independent price with the front-office mark. Input IPV compares curves, volatilities or other inputs, often by repricing the same position. Keep market-data differences distinct from model differences: otherwise a model fault can be hidden in a price exception.',
'A zero difference is evidence only if the comparator is credible. Copying the front-office mark into the independent field produces a perfect match and no independent assurance. Track independence, coverage and evidence quality alongside differences.'
],example:{title:'A typical monthly control chain',text:'Population → source validation → normalisation\n→ independent price or revaluation\n→ difference → exception investigation\n→ approved action → reconciliation → sign-off',explain:'This is a practical learning workflow, not a prescribed organisation chart. Product control, valuation control, finance and model risk responsibilities vary by bank.'},deep:'Possible outcomes include “verified”, “requires investigation”, “unverified due to insufficient evidence”, “mark corrected”, or “adjustment approved”. Missing evidence must not become a zero difference. Coverage measures should distinguish verified, proxy-verified, unverified and excluded positions; document why exclusions are legitimate.',data:'Keep RunId, PositionSnapshotId, SourceSnapshotId, IndependentPrice, RepricedValue, Difference, EvidenceStatus, ExceptionOwner, Resolution and ApprovalId. Store original observations as well as the selected result.',pitfall:'IPV success does not prove the absence of valuation uncertainty. A well-supported mid can still have close-out costs.',quiz:{q:'A feed is missing, so the job defaults the independent price to the FO price. The result is zero. Is it verified?',options:['Yes, the values match','Only if notional is small','No, missing evidence must remain visible'],correct:2,explain:'The fallback hides a coverage failure. Preserve a missing/unverified status and route it for review.'},sources:['basel'],lab:'ipv'},
{id:5,phase:'Valuation control',title:'Sources, tolerances and exceptions',minutes:11,teaser:'Learn what makes a price usable and a difference actionable.',take:'A tolerance prioritises investigation; it does not certify that a valuation is correct.',body:[
'Source quality is multidimensional: relevance to the instrument, timestamp, executability, size, independence, number of contributors and whether the market is active. A consensus price can be useful, but ten contributors repeating the same stale information are not ten independent observations.',
'A practical source preference might start with relevant transactions or executable quotes and then use evaluated prices, consensus, proxies or expert judgement. This is not a universal ranking: a current, well-matched evaluated price may be more relevant than a tiny stale transaction.',
'An exception can arise from a currency impact, a price/input threshold or a data-quality failure. Record each trigger separately. A toy rule in this hub flags a monetary difference over a configurable tolerance; an unreliable source separately produces “unverified”. These are learning rules, not industry-wide limits.',
'Investigate the cause before the accounting action: incorrect position, timestamp mismatch, clean/dirty mismatch, stale source, different curves, real price disagreement or a model problem. Retain both signed impact and absolute magnitude so favourable and adverse differences cannot disappear through netting.'
],example:{title:'Same price difference, very different impact',text:'FO 99.80; independent 99.65\nDifference = −0.15 price points\n$2m long face   → −$3,000\n$20m long face  → −$30,000\n$20m short face → +$30,000',explain:'Signed notional determines the P&L direction. Absolute impact can be useful for triage; it is not the signed adjustment to book.'},deep:'A robust exception record answers: what failed, how much is affected, which evidence was used, who investigated, what decision was made and when it expires. Ageing matters: recurring exceptions may reveal a structural coverage gap. Overrides need a reason, an approver and an expiry or review date. A source hierarchy should be versioned so a rerun is explainable.',data:'An INNER JOIN against the price feed silently drops unmatched positions. Preserve the position population with a left join, explicit missing flags and row-count/value reconciliation. Deduplicate quotes using source identifiers and timestamps before selecting a price.',pitfall:'Do not average every quote blindly. A stale quote, a different currency or a different seniority is not an equally plausible observation.',quiz:{q:'A $30k difference is below an internal $50k threshold, but the independent quote is six weeks stale. What follows?',options:['Pass: below threshold means correct','Investigate source quality independently of the amount','Use zero as the independent price'],correct:1,explain:'Numerical materiality and evidence quality are separate controls. A stale comparator can invalidate the test itself.'},sources:['basel','eu'],lab:'ipv'},
{id:6,phase:'Valuation control',title:'Reserves and the accounting value bridge',minutes:10,teaser:'Keep mark corrections, reserves and movements separate.',take:'A valuation adjustment has a reason, a basis, a sign and a scope. “Reserve” alone does not tell you what it does.',body:[
'Banks use “reserve” in different ways. Here, an accounting valuation adjustment changes the value recognised in the accounts to reflect the applicable measurement basis. A mark correction fixes an identified wrong mark. A reserve may address uncertainty or an aspect not already captured. Both require a clear basis and must avoid overlap.',
'For a long asset in our examples, a downward adjustment is shown as a positive deduction: accounting fair value = raw value − deductions. Ledger systems may instead store the adjustment as a signed negative number. For liabilities, cash flows and own-credit effects, a generic “always subtract” rule is unsafe.',
'Reserve balance and reserve movement are different fields. If a deduction rises from $20k to $35k, the incremental negative valuation effect is $15k, not $35k again. Releasing a deduction can create a positive movement; explain why the underlying need changed.',
'Accounting presentation can involve P&L or other comprehensive income depending on the instrument and classification. A regulatory AVA belongs to the capital calculation rather than automatically becoming an additional accounting journal.'
],example:{title:'A bridge that reconciles',text:'Raw clean value          $10,200,000\nApproved price correction  −$100,000\nBid-offer adjustment        −$60,000\nModel adjustment            −$40,000\nAccounting fair value    $10,000,000',explain:'The amounts are synthetic and assumed non-overlapping. An actual book must establish whether the raw mark already includes any of these effects.'},deep:'A reserve engine needs stock, movement and attribution. Opening balance + new positions + market change + methodology change + FX translation + releases + other approved effects should reconcile to closing balance under defined sign conventions. Attribution is often approximate when inputs interact; a residual should be visible rather than silently forced to zero.',data:'AdjustmentId, Type, ScopeKey, CalculationBasis, Currency, SignedAmount, EffectiveDate, Reason, ModelVersion and ApprovalId make the bridge auditable. Keep position-level allocations separate from the original portfolio-level calculation.',pitfall:'A portfolio reserve allocation is not necessarily a stand-alone reserve calculation for each trade. Summing allocated and original portfolio amounts double counts.',quiz:{q:'A long-asset reserve deduction increases from $20k to $35k. With all else unchanged, the valuation movement is…',options:['−$35k','+$15k','−$15k'],correct:2,explain:'The balance changed by $15k. The increased deduction reduces value by that amount.'},sources:['ifrs','qna'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:7,phase:'Valuation control',title:'Bid-offer, liquidity and model uncertainty',minutes:10,teaser:'Distinguish the reasons behind adjustments.',take:'Name the uncertainty before calculating an adjustment. Similar-looking deductions can measure different things.',body:[
'Bid-offer captures the difference between a reference mark and an appropriate exit basis. Liquidity concerns include the availability and depth of trading. Concentration adds the issue of position size relative to market capacity. These can interact, so separate labels alone do not prove that adjustments are independent.',
'Model uncertainty arises when plausible modelling choices produce different values. A programming defect is different: a known implementation error should be corrected and its effects assessed. Reserving for a known defect without fixing it is not a durable control.',
'CVA reflects counterparty credit effects in derivative valuation; DVA reflects own-credit effects. Funding valuation adjustments address relevant funding economics under the methodology used. Their calculation can depend on netting, collateral, future exposure and funding assumptions. They are not simply fixed percentages of notional.',
'A valuation adjustment is not the same as a capital requirement for the risk of its future movement. In particular, a CVA amount and a regulatory CVA risk capital charge answer different questions. Also distinguish loan-loss allowances from the fair-value reserves studied here.'
],example:{title:'Ask a different question for each line',text:'Bid-offer: What exit side is appropriate?\nModel: Which plausible valuation is defensible?\nConcentration: How long and how costly to unwind?\nCredit: What does non-performance do to value?',explain:'Use the questions as a map. You still need the applicable policy, unit of account and evidence before recording an amount.'},deep:'A model comparison should hold other conditions constant if the goal is isolating model uncertainty. Repricing with a different model and a different market snapshot mixes two effects. For options, interpolation and extrapolation can matter even when the headline volatility input looks identical. For portfolios, netting may alter close-out or credit calculations; never infer netting eligibility from a shared customer name.',data:'NettingSetId, CollateralAgreementId and LegalEntity may be essential calculation keys. A broken mapping can affect CVA/FVA or close-out results even if all trade prices look correct.',pitfall:'Do not apply a blanket accounting concentration discount to an unadjusted Level 1 price. Prudential concentration assessment is a separate exercise.',quiz:{q:'Two model implementations differ because one contains an identified cash-flow bug. What is the best classification?',options:['A defect requiring correction and impact assessment','An unavoidable market spread','Automatic Level 3 classification'],correct:0,explain:'Known errors are not simply alternative defensible model assumptions. Fix the defect and assess affected valuations.'},sources:['ifrsdetail','basel','eu'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:8,phase:'Fair value hierarchy',title:'Level 1, Level 2, Level 3',minutes:9,teaser:'Classify the evidence supporting the valuation.',take:'Fair Value Hierarchy classifies the measurement by the significance and observability of its inputs, not by product name or code complexity.',body:[
'Level 1 uses unadjusted quoted prices for identical instruments in active markets. Level 2 uses observable inputs other than qualifying Level 1 prices. Level 3 involves an unobservable input significant to the overall measurement. When inputs span levels, the lowest significant input level determines the overall classification.',
'An exchange-traded share with a qualifying unadjusted quote is a natural Level 1 example. A vanilla swap valued from observable curves is a common Level 2 example. A structured derivative whose value materially depends on unobservable long-term correlation can be Level 3.',
'These are conditional examples, not product lookup rules. An exchange listing does not by itself demonstrate an active market. A vendor-provided price is not automatically Level 2. A sophisticated model can still use observable inputs, and a simple instrument can depend on a significant unobservable input.',
'Classification helps explain how the number was obtained. It is not a direct credit rating, liquidity score, probability of loss or rule for how much AVA to calculate.'
],example:{title:'One important input can change the classification',text:'Discount curve       observable\nFX forwards          observable\nLong-term correlation unobservable\nCorrelation significant to total value? Yes\nIllustrative outcome: Level 3',explain:'Counting observable inputs misses the point. Ninety-nine observable inputs do not cancel one significant unobservable input.'},deep:'Review the measurement as a whole. A significant adjustment to an observable input can change the hierarchy outcome when it introduces unobservable assumptions. Record the assessment as of the reporting date, including the evidence supporting market activity and input availability. Transfers between levels need reasons and the relevant reporting policy.',data:'Keep InputObservability separate from OverallFVLevel. Store SignificanceAssessment, EvidenceDate and ClassificationReason. A single vendor flag rarely supplies the whole classification rationale.',pitfall:'FVH is not calculated by averaging levels: Level 1 + Level 3 does not give Level 2.',quiz:{q:'A complex swap model uses only relevant observable significant inputs, without a qualifying Level 1 quote. Its likely level is…',options:['Level 1 because the inputs exist','Level 2','Level 3 because the code is complex'],correct:1,explain:'The evidence and input significance determine the hierarchy, not the length of the model code.'},sources:['ifrsdetail'],lab:'fvh'},
{id:9,phase:'Fair value hierarchy',title:'Observability and significance in practice',minutes:10,teaser:'Move from a flag to a defensible assessment.',take:'“Unobservable” describes the evidence. “Significant” describes the effect on the whole measurement. You need both.',body:[
'An input may be well supported at a five-year tenor and weakly supported at twenty years. Extrapolating a curve beyond available market evidence introduces assumptions even when the earlier curve points are observable. A price source name cannot resolve this alone.',
'One way to understand significance is to change the uncertain input across a defensible range and examine the effect on the complete value. That does not create a universal numerical threshold. Banks establish assessment methods and governance, and qualitative factors can matter.',
'For technology work, distinguish missing data from an approved unobservable estimate. Missing is a data/control state; an estimated input can be an intentional methodological choice with documentation. Treating null as zero collapses that distinction.',
'Classification can change when markets become inactive, a significant input loses observable support, or new evidence becomes available. A transfer is not necessarily a trade event. It can be an evidence event.'
],example:{title:'A useful sensitivity question',text:'Base structured-note value  $2.00m\nValue at low correlation    $1.72m\nValue at high correlation   $2.21m\nEvidence for correlation    internal estimate',explain:'The range suggests that correlation deserves a serious significance assessment. It does not prove a universal percentage cut-off for Level 3.'},deep:'Document the alternative inputs, why the range is plausible, how the valuation was rerun, the measure used to assess significance and who approved the conclusion. Near-zero net values can make percentage tests misleading: a $10k change relative to a $1k net value is huge, even though the gross cash-flow legs may be much larger. Avoid invented automatic thresholds.',data:'Store observability by input, tenor and valuation date. Version overrides and reference the evidence. A portfolio-level “Level 2” flag copied from the previous month can conceal a newly significant unobservable input.',pitfall:'“Quoted by a broker” and “observable for this valuation” are not synonyms. Relevance and support matter.',quiz:{q:'An unobservable input exists but is not significant to the overall measurement. Does its mere presence force Level 3?',options:['Yes, any unobservable input does','No; significance must be assessed','No; all broker inputs are Level 1'],correct:1,explain:'The overall hierarchy follows the lowest significant input level, not every immaterial input.'},sources:['ifrsdetail'],lab:'fvh'},
{id:10,phase:'Fair value hierarchy',title:'FVH classification and reporting exercises',minutes:16,teaser:'Explain the level, the evidence and what could change it.',take:'A useful classification record explains the decision and its evidence, rather than storing only “2” or “3”.',body:[
'Consider a bond valued using comparable bonds and an observable issuer spread. If the significant inputs are observable and there is no qualifying identical active-market quote, Level 2 can be appropriate. If an important spread must instead be estimated without market support, the outcome may change.',
'Consider an illiquid listed share. The word “listed” is not enough to establish Level 1. Investigate market activity and how the value is actually measured. Conversely, a Level 3 position is not automatically mispriced: its valuation relies on significant judgement that must be explained.',
'Reporting usually requires reconciling the hierarchy population to accounting fair values, with consistent currency conversion and scope. A total of trade notionals is not a fair-value disclosure total. Netting and presentation must follow the relevant reporting rules.',
'Explain the proposed level, its supporting evidence and a fact that would change the classification.'
],example:{title:'Explain a classification in one sentence',text:'“Level 3 because the unobservable long-term\nvolatility assumption materially affects the\noverall measurement; review approved under\nevidence pack EV-102.”',explain:'This is much more useful than “Level 3 because exotic”. It connects an input, a valuation effect and a controlled decision.'},deep:'Do not confuse an FVH transfer with a reserve movement. Both may be caused by deteriorating evidence, but one changes a classification and the other changes a measured amount. A classification change can happen with no immediate value change. Keep the two event types distinct in reporting and movement explanations.',data:'Reconcile sum of accounting FV by currency/entity and level against the defined accounting population. Investigate unmapped levels, duplicate classifications, out-of-period overrides and stale evidence. Preserve the timing policy for transfers.',pitfall:'If the system assigns Level 3 only when reserve > 0, it is encoding the wrong relationship. Observability and adjustment amount are separate dimensions.',quiz:{q:'A position moves from Level 2 to Level 3. Must its fair value immediately decrease?',options:['Yes, Level 3 always means a loss','No; classification and value are different outputs','Yes, by 10%'],correct:1,explain:'Changed evidence can alter the hierarchy without changing the best current value estimate.'},sources:['ifrsdetail'],lab:'fvh'},
{id:11,phase:'Prudent valuation',title:'PruVal, AVAs and capital',minutes:10,teaser:'Separate the accounting number from the prudential overlay.',take:'Prudent valuation addresses uncertainty for regulatory capital. An AVA is additional to the relevant uncertainty already recognised in accounting value.',body:[
'Accounting and prudential measurement serve different purposes. In the EU framework, prudent valuation applies to relevant fair-valued positions in both trading and non-trading books. AVAs reduce Common Equity Tier 1 capital under the CRR. They do not simply overwrite the accounting fair value.',
'CET1 is a core regulatory capital measure with prescribed inclusions and deductions. It is not cash sitting in a separate account. If CET1 decreases while risk-weighted assets stay fixed, the CET1 ratio decreases. A valuation change and a capital-ratio change are therefore related but different outputs.',
'Begin conceptually with raw value, identify accounting adjustments already captured and assess the remaining prudential uncertainty. Offset only an adjustment demonstrably addressing the same uncertainty at the appropriate calculation level. Never use an unrelated reserve as a general credit against every AVA.',
'The general concepts are useful across jurisdictions. The thresholds, implementation details, reporting forms and effective dates must come from the regime and entity that actually apply to your bank.'
],example:{title:'Keep the starting points straight',text:'Unadjusted asset value     €10.2m\nSame-source accounting adj  €0.2m\nAccounting fair value      €10.0m\nTotal prudent deduction     €0.5m\nIncremental deduction       €0.3m',explain:'The €0.3m is a schematic residual before any applicable aggregation. Starting from the already-adjusted €10.0m and deducting €0.5m again would double count €0.2m.'},deep:'The teaching bridge uses a long asset and positive deduction magnitudes. Production AVA methodologies can operate on valuation exposures or portfolios with hedges and permitted aggregation. Do not apply the bridge independently to every trade and assume summing those outputs reproduces the regulatory total.',data:'AccountingFV, RecognisedSameSourceAdjustment, GrossPrudentRequirement, AVABeforeAggregation and AggregatedAVA are distinct concepts. The mapping needs policy version, jurisdiction, reporting date and calculation scope.',pitfall:'AVA is not a synonym for IPV difference, accounting reserve, CVA or market-risk capital charge.',quiz:{q:'A €500k prudent deduction is required from a raw mark. A €200k same-source accounting deduction is already included. The schematic incremental amount is…',options:['€700k','€500k','€300k'],correct:2,explain:'€500k − €200k = €300k, floored at zero. This is before the applicable aggregation methodology.'},sources:['crr','eu','qna'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:12,phase:'Prudent valuation',title:'Market price uncertainty',minutes:18,teaser:'Separate uncertainty about the mark from market risk tomorrow.',take:'MPU concerns uncertainty around today’s valuation inputs or price, rather than a forecast of tomorrow’s market loss.',body:[
'Several credible sources can disagree even at the same time. That spread of evidence suggests uncertainty about the current mark. Before interpreting it, align instrument, side, size, currency, time and convention, and assess dependence between sources.',
'The EU core framework uses a 90% certainty objective for the prudent point in the plausible range: price or better. For a long asset, the adverse direction is lower; for a liability or a short, it reverses. It is not a rule to take the 90th percentile of prices in every case.',
'The uncertainty lab uses an explicitly assumed normal distribution and a one-sided 1.28155 standard-deviation multiplier. It is a mathematical teaching device for direction and spread. A handful of dealer quotes does not establish a valid statistical distribution or regulatory calibration.',
'Data-poor situations need governed expert judgement rather than false precision. Document why the range is plausible, which positions it represents, how sensitivities translate it into value and what evidence could improve the estimate.'
],example:{title:'Direction matters',text:'Assumed central price       100.00\nAssumed standard deviation    0.20\nToy lower 10th percentile    99.7437\nToy upper 90th percentile   100.2563',explain:'Under this invented normal model, 90% of the price distribution lies above the lower point. That illustrates a long-asset adverse direction; it does not prove a real-world 90% exit guarantee.'},deep:'Market risk usually studies changes over a future horizon. Valuation uncertainty asks what price or input is supportable now. The two can interact in stressed markets, but a VaR/expected-shortfall number is not a substitute for an MPU AVA. Netting valuation exposures requires methodological support; opposing notionals alone do not prove the same uncertain input is hedged.',data:'SourceRange, DataQuality, InputUncertainty, ValuationExposure, HedgeMapping, ExpertOverlay and CalibrationVersion should be traceable. Preserve rejected source observations and reasons so the selected range can be challenged.',pitfall:'The 90% target is not a universal 10% haircut, and an observed minimum from five quotes is not automatically the required quantile.',quiz:{q:'For a long asset, which direction is generally prudent when price uncertainty is being assessed?',options:['Higher price','Lower price','Always the mean'],correct:1,explain:'A lower asset value is adverse. For a short or liability the direction reverses. The distribution and methodology still need evidence.'},sources:['eu'],lab:'uncertainty'},
{id:13,phase:'Prudent valuation',title:'Close-out cost and concentration',minutes:10,teaser:'Connect spread, size and time to exit.',take:'A mid-price estimate and the cost of exiting a position answer different questions.',body:[
'Close-out cost captures costs associated with the bid-offer spread in the relevant valuation exposure. In the EU core framework, the prudent spread assessment uses the certainty objective and normally applies half the assessed full spread, with recognition of applicable existing adjustments. Do not subtract the full bid-to-ask spread from a long position already at mid.',
'Concentration concerns positions that may require an extended period to exit. Market capacity, position size, uncertainty during the exit period and an exit strategy can matter. It is not enough to divide notional by daily volume and call the result an AVA.',
'Hedged risks can have different close-out behaviour from standalone positions. You need a defined valuation exposure and justified netting or offset assumptions. The actual cost of unwinding a portfolio is not always the sum of costs of closing each trade independently.',
'The relationship with accounting adjustments must be explicit. An accounting bid-offer reserve can already cover part of the same uncertainty. A separate model reserve cannot automatically offset close-out cost.'
],example:{title:'Half-spread from a mid mark',text:'Bid / ask                99.80 / 100.20\nFull spread              0.40 points\nMid-to-bid               0.20 points\nLong face                $10m\nIllustrative exit cost   $20k',explain:'A gross prudent close-out requirement of $30k and an eligible same-source booked deduction of $20k leave a schematic $10k increment before applicable aggregation.'},deep:'A quoted spread may apply to a small clip rather than your full position. An exit strategy can involve staged trading or hedging, with costs dependent on market conditions. Treat a participation-rate calculation as an operational scenario, not a prescribed regulatory formula. Accounting restrictions on blockage factors must remain separate from prudential concentration rules.',data:'ExposureGroup, SpreadConvention (full/half), QuoteSize, PositionSize, MarketVolume, ExitHorizon and AlreadyBookedAmount are common sources of errors. A full-spread/half-spread mix-up doubles a result.',pitfall:'If a mark is already bid-based, a further mid-to-bid deduction can duplicate an existing effect.',quiz:{q:'Bid is 99, ask is 101, and the mark is mid at 100. What is the simple mid-to-bid distance?',options:['2 price points','1 price point','0.5 price points'],correct:1,explain:'The full spread is 2; the half-spread from mid to bid is 1.'},sources:['eu','ifrsdetail'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:14,phase:'Prudent valuation',title:'Model risk and the other AVA categories',minutes:12,teaser:'Know all nine categories and their overlaps.',take:'Nine category names form a coverage map. They do not mean nine independent percentages should be added to every trade.',body:[
'Model risk examines uncertainty from defensible alternative models or calibrations. In the EU framework, market-derived input uncertainty already addressed by MPU is excluded from model-risk treatment to avoid overlap. A review should distinguish uncertain assumptions from known defects.',
'Unearned credit spreads and investing/funding costs address valuation uncertainty in credit and funding adjustments. Their uncertainty is allocated through market price uncertainty, close-out cost and model risk under the EU RTS. They are not extra duplicate buckets to stack on top of those same amounts.',
'Concentrated positions address the challenge of exiting size. Future administrative costs concern relevant future servicing/administration under the regulatory conditions. Early termination concerns the effect of non-contractual termination behaviour. Operational-risk AVA addresses valuation-process operational uncertainty under the specified approach.',
'Applicability and prescribed treatment matter. You cannot assume each category is material for each product, or that a zero result means no assessment is required. The sources panel provides the legal articles for deeper study.'
],example:{title:'The nine-category map',text:'1 Market price uncertainty    2 Close-out costs\n3 Model risk                  4 Unearned credit spreads\n5 Investing / funding costs   6 Concentrated positions\n7 Future administrative costs 8 Early termination\n9 Operational risk',explain:'Articles 9–17 of EU 2016/101 cover these areas. Categories 4 and 5 feed the uncertainty categories under their specified treatment; avoid duplicate totals.'},deep:'For an illustrative model-risk discussion, hold trade data and market snapshot fixed: Model A values a long asset at 12.4m and Model B at 11.8m. The 0.6m difference identifies a question; it is not automatically the correct reserve or AVA. Assess model validity, plausible alternatives, the selected prudent point, what is already booked and the permitted aggregation. Article 17 of the referenced EU RTS still mentions an Advanced Measurement Approach (AMA) zero condition and otherwise prescribes 10% of aggregated MPU plus close-out cost AVAs. CRR3 has since removed AMA for operational-risk capital. Do not treat that old zero condition as a current general option; check the current supervisory interpretation. EBA question 2025_7506 was rejected without a substantive answer, so it does not resolve the mismatch.',data:'Use an uncertainty taxonomy and overlap mapping. Record a gross uncertainty assessment separately from the final reporting bucket. A data model with nine unrelated columns can obscure required allocation and reconciliation.',pitfall:'CVA is not the same as unearned-credit-spread AVA. One is a valuation component; the other addresses prudential uncertainty around relevant valuation components.',quiz:{q:'Why is simply adding separate credit/funding AVAs to MPU, close-out and model risk potentially wrong?',options:['Credit has no valuation uncertainty','Their uncertainties can already be allocated into those categories','All nine categories always equal zero'],correct:1,explain:'The RTS prescribes how these uncertainties feed other categories. A naive sum can count the same uncertainty twice.'},sources:['eu','crr3','opq'],lab:'bridge'},
{id:15,phase:'Prudent valuation',title:'Approaches, aggregation and CET1',minutes:25,teaser:'Understand the regulatory machinery without mistaking it for a toy calculator.',take:'Scope and aggregation are part of the methodology. A correct trade formula can still produce the wrong regulatory total.',body:[
'Under the referenced EU RTS, an eligible simplified approach uses 0.1% of the defined aggregate absolute fair-valued asset and liability amount, with a €15bn threshold and specific eligibility/scope rules. It is not 0.1% of derivative notional. Individual and consolidated eligibility both matter.',
'The core approach calculates valuation uncertainty by category with prescribed aggregation. Only permitted offsets and diversification belong in that calculation. The first three categories have Annex methods; do not apply a generic “halve the total AVA” instruction to all nine categories.',
'For Annex Method 1, APVA = (1 − alpha) × (FV − PV), using the method’s definitions and required prior treatment. Alpha is 50% in the referenced post-2020 baseline. Method 2 also uses an expected value: max[0, FV − alpha × EV − (1 − alpha) × PV]. These are method-specific aggregation expressions, not universal trade-level bridge formulas.',
'The UK has separate rules; its simplified threshold is £13bn in the referenced instrument. As of this edition, the PRA’s January 2026 restatement has relevant provisions effective from January 2027. Keep publication date, effective date and reporting date distinct.'
],example:{title:'Translate an AVA into a ratio effect',text:'CET1 before AVA       €1,000m\nFinal AVA deduction       €3m\nCET1 after AVA          €997m\nRisk-weighted assets  €10,000m\nRatio before / after  10.00% / 9.97%',explain:'This holds every other capital item and RWA fixed. The 0.03 percentage-point change equals 3 basis points of capital ratio, not a 3% drop in the ratio.'},deep:'The referenced EU calculation is at least quarterly. A bank may run internal estimates more often. Exactly matching offsets, partial CET1 effects and consolidation rules make the eligible base more nuanced than a gross ledger total. Consultation proposals do not amend production rules until adopted and effective. The app’s bridge deliberately stops before regulatory aggregation; the source provides the complete conditions.',data:'Regime, RuleVersion, EffectiveFrom, ReportingDate, ConsolidationScope, CET1ImpactProportion, AggregationMethod and Category need controlled definitions. Aggregate currencies only after an approved translation process; do not sum EUR and USD amounts as if they were the same unit.',pitfall:'Do not use a current EU threshold for a UK entity or implement a consultation as if it were enacted law.',quiz:{q:'Which base is conceptually relevant to the EU simplified 0.1% calculation?',options:['All derivative notionals','The defined eligible aggregate absolute fair-valued assets and liabilities','Only Level 3 assets'],correct:1,explain:'The eligible fair-value base, scope adjustments and entity/group eligibility rules matter. It is not a notional or Level 3-only calculation.'},sources:['eu','crr','uk','uk2027','consult'],lab:'capital'},
{id:16,phase:'Working knowledge',title:'Day-one P&L and valuation movements',minutes:10,teaser:'Understand why a model gain may not be recognised immediately.',take:'A model can imply a gain on day one without proving that the gain is immediately recognisable in the accounts.',body:[
'Suppose the bank pays 100 to acquire an asset its model values at 103. From the bank’s perspective, the apparent 3-unit gain invites a question: does observable market evidence support the difference, or is it created by assumptions that cannot be independently substantiated?',
'IFRS 9 distinguishes differences evidenced by qualifying quoted prices or a valuation using only observable market data from other differences. In the latter case, the difference is deferred under the prescribed conditions, with later recognition linked to relevant changes including time. This is more specific than “every Level 3 trade has a day-one reserve”.',
'For the opposite perspective, a bank issuing a liability with fair value 103 for cash proceeds 100 has an apparent 3-unit loss, not a gain. Issuing a liability with fair value 100 for proceeds 103 gives an apparent gain of 3. Identify the bank’s asset or liability and cash direction before applying the recognition or deferral test.',
'After day one, explain valuation changes through market movements, new trades, maturities, cash flows, FX, model changes and adjustment movements. A value decrease is not necessarily a loss on its own if a coupon or settlement cash flow was paid during the period.',
'For systems work, a deferred difference needs a lifecycle: original amount, remaining balance, recognition events and supporting evidence. A fixed amortisation schedule should not be invented merely because it is easy to implement.'
],example:{title:'Distinguish value change from economic return',text:'Opening dirty value  $1,010,000\nClosing dirty value  $1,005,000\nCoupon received          $8,000\nSimplified total return  $3,000',explain:'$1,005,000 − $1,010,000 + $8,000 = $3,000, before other trades, expenses or FX. Ignoring the coupon would suggest a misleading $5,000 loss.'},deep:'A reserve release, a day-one deferral release and an IPV correction can all affect reported movements but have different causes. Keep them separate in attribution. A method change may produce a one-off movement; compare the same population under old and new methods to isolate it from market and trade changes.',data:'OpeningValue, ClosingValue, CashFlows, TradeActivity, FXEffect, ModelChange, ReserveMovement and UnexplainedResidual support a movement bridge. For deferrals, include OriginalDifference, RemainingDeferredAmount, ReleaseReason and Approval.',pitfall:'Do not use Level 3 as an automatic proxy for the IFRS 9 day-one recognition test.',quiz:{q:'The bank buys an asset for 100 and models its fair value at 103 using a significant unsupported input. Is the apparent 3-unit gain automatically recognised?',options:['Yes: every model gain goes straight to profit','No: apply the IFRS 9 day-one evidence test and prescribed deferral treatment','No: any Level 2 instrument must always be deferred'],correct:1,explain:'The apparent gain is 3 from the bank’s asset perspective, but arithmetic does not establish recognition. Apply B5.1.2A; a hierarchy label alone does not replace its evidence test.'},sources:['ifrs9'],lab:'bond'},
{id:17,phase:'Working knowledge',title:'Data lineage, controls and debugging',minutes:15,teaser:'Trace a number from market feed to report.',take:'A reliable valuation result must be reproducible from a defined position snapshot, evidence snapshot, methodology and approved decisions.',body:[
'Imagine a user says: “MPU doubled overnight.” Start by asking whether the population, inputs, method, offsets, currency conversion or reporting mapping changed. Re-running the latest data does not reproduce yesterday’s result if the source snapshots have been overwritten.',
'Build a chain from trade capture to positions, normalised market data, model valuation, independent comparison, exceptions, accounting adjustments, hierarchy assessment, prudential assessment and reporting. Some steps branch rather than occur strictly in series: FVH classifies the measurement, and model governance supports the whole chain.',
'Useful controls include completeness reconciliations, duplicate detection, valid units, stale-data checks, sign checks, source independence, mapping coverage and bridge reconciliation. Technical job success means the program ran; it does not mean the population or the valuation is correct.',
'Make reruns idempotent: the same inputs and version should produce the same logical results without duplicate postings. Late data should trigger a controlled revised run, with an explanation of what changed, rather than silently altering a signed-off report.'
],example:{title:'Debug from the outside in',text:'1 Compare run metadata and row counts\n2 Reconcile notionals and values by book/currency\n3 Compare changed source observations\n4 Inspect method and override versions\n5 Reprice a representative exception\n6 Reconcile results to the report',explain:'This order can distinguish a population problem from a valuation problem before you spend hours inside the pricing function.'},deep:'Use stable business keys plus run identifiers. Many-to-many joins can multiply a position when there are several quotes or classifications per instrument. Reduce each source to the intended as-of record before joining, or explicitly retain the one-to-many evidence relation. Money calculations need controlled decimal precision and documented rounding; the browser’s floating-point labs are educational, not ledger engines.',data:'Recommended lineage keys: RunId, PositionSnapshotId, SourceSnapshotId, InstrumentId, ValuationDate, MethodVersion, OverrideId, ApprovalId and ReportingBatchId. Include source timestamps and time zones, not just local date strings.',pitfall:'An unmatched row that disappears can improve your apparent exception rate. Reconcile coverage denominators, not just exception counts.',quiz:{q:'The same source files are rerun and duplicate reserve journals appear. Which property is missing?',options:['Idempotency','Volatility','A lower tolerance'],correct:0,explain:'An idempotent process avoids creating duplicate logical outputs or postings for the same authorised calculation.'},sources:['basel'],lab:'ipv'},
{id:18,phase:'Working knowledge',title:'Put it all together at month-end',minutes:15,teaser:'Explain a full position without mixing the outputs.',take:'You understand the workflow when you can explain the value, the evidence, the adjustments, the hierarchy and the capital effect separately.',body:[
'Take the long bond in the month-end case. Its FO clean price is 99.80, the selected independent clean mid is 99.65 and face amount is $20m. The signed IPV difference is −$30k. In the case, an investigation concludes that the FO mark should be corrected. That conclusion is a supplied scenario fact, not something the subtraction alone proves.',
'After correction, the clean value is $19.93m. An approved $10k bid-offer deduction gives accounting clean fair value of $19.92m. Add $40k fixed accrued interest for the case’s accounting dirty value of $19.96m. The case classifies the measurement as Level 2 because the assumed significant inputs are observable and there is no qualifying Level 1 quote.',
'A supplied gross prudent close-out requirement of $16k with a $10k same-source booked deduction leaves $6k incremental close-out assessment before aggregation. A separate supplied $8k MPU requirement has no same-source booked offset in this scenario. The diagnostic pre-aggregation total is $14k; it is not a final regulatory AVA.',
'Now explain what would change each result: a different position size changes currency impact, a stale quote changes evidence status, a significant unobservable input can change hierarchy, and an eligible booked adjustment can change the incremental prudential amount. These relationships are more useful than memorising the final numbers.'
],example:{title:'Five outputs, five questions',text:'IPV difference   −$30k → Does the mark agree?\nAccounting FV   $19.96m → What is booked, dirty?\nHierarchy       Level 2 → What evidence supports it?\nPre-agg MPU       $8k → What uncertainty remains?\nPre-agg COC       $6k → What exit uncertainty remains?',explain:'Use the full case to reveal each step, inspect the sample data and practise explaining the result without seeing the answer first.'},deep:'A strong work explanation names the baseline, signs, units and calculation level. It says which amounts are scenario assumptions and which are calculated. It also explains unresolved evidence. “The result is $14k” is incomplete without “diagnostic pre-aggregation, not final AVA, USD, this valuation exposure, these same-source offsets”.',data:'The supplied CSV includes a clean/dirty convention, price evidence, signed quantity and scenario assumptions. Rows intentionally include a stale source, missing source and short position. Treat missing inputs as missing; do not coerce them to zero.',pitfall:'The $30k price correction fixes the central mark in this case. It is not automatically an eligible offset against the separate residual MPU assessment.',quiz:{q:'Can the case’s $14k pre-aggregation total be reported as the final regulatory AVA without further methodology?',options:['Yes, the arithmetic is correct','No, applicable scope and aggregation still matter','Yes, because the bond is Level 2'],correct:1,explain:'Correct arithmetic is necessary but the regulatory method also specifies scope, eligibility, offsets and aggregation.'},sources:['eu','basel','ifrsdetail'],lab:'ipv'}
];
// Optional changed-case practice extends the existing reading flow.
LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===3).deep += ' For a changed-case calculation, open valuation-transfer.md in the swap-repricing practice files. Predict a 10 bp forward bump before running the test, then fix that rate and explain why the forecast bump stops changing the cash flow.';
LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===17).deep += ' A zero aggregate difference can conceal different errors: duplicating a 100-unit position and dropping another 100-unit position preserves both total value and row count. Reconcile identifiers and multiplicity as well as amounts. Change one identifier in a copied synthetic fixture and locate the mismatch before recalculating totals.';

const GLOSSARY = [
['Accrued interest','Interest earned since the last coupon date but not yet paid. Dirty bond value includes it; clean price excludes it.'],['Accounting fair value','The recognised fair-value measurement under the applicable accounting framework, including relevant adjustments.'],['Adjustment / reserve','A valuation change with a defined reason and basis. “Reserve” is bank-specific terminology; identify whether it is accounting, prudential or another item.'],['Aggregation','Combining calculation components under defined rules, including only permitted offsets or diversification.'],['AVA','Additional Valuation Adjustment: a prudential increment for valuation uncertainty under the applicable framework. Distinct from an accounting journal.'],['Basis point (bp)','0.01 percentage point; 0.0001 as a decimal. For a per-100 bond quote, 0.01 price point corresponds to 1bp of face value, not necessarily 1bp of yield.'],['Bid / ask / mid','Bid is a dealer’s buying price; ask is their selling price; mid is their midpoint. Always specify perspective.'],['Calibration','Selecting model parameters so a model fits chosen observations or objectives.'],['CET1','Common Equity Tier 1: a regulatory capital measure after prescribed inclusions and deductions.'],['Clean / dirty','Clean bond price excludes accrued interest; dirty includes it. Compare prices on the same basis.'],['Close-out cost (COC)','Cost or uncertainty associated with exiting a valuation exposure, often linked to bid-offer.'],['Concentration','Position size relative to market capacity and the practical difficulty of exit.'],['Consensus price','A price derived from multiple submitted observations; quality depends on contributor independence and relevance.'],['Correlation','A measure of joint movement. Long-dated or specific correlations may lack observable market support.'],['CVA / DVA','Credit / Debit Valuation Adjustment: counterparty-credit / own-credit effects in valuation. Not the same as CVA risk capital.'],['Day-one difference','Difference between transaction price and model fair value at inception; recognition or deferral follows specific accounting criteria.'],['Discount factor','The multiplier that converts a future cash flow to present value.'],['DV01 / PV01','Value sensitivity to a 1bp rate move. Sign, curve scope and unit conventions must be checked.'],['Evidence status','Whether a usable independent source exists and meets the required quality criteria. Separate from the size of a price difference.'],['Exit price','A market-participant perspective on selling an asset or transferring a liability at the measurement date.'],['Fair Value Hierarchy (FVH)','Level 1/2/3 classification of the measurement based on qualifying quotes and significant input observability.'],['FVA','Funding Valuation Adjustment. The term and methodology need context; it is not a universal notional percentage.'],['Idempotency','Rerunning the same logical operation does not create duplicate business effects, such as extra reserve postings.'],['IPV','Independent Price Verification: independent challenge of prices or model inputs and investigation of supporting evidence.'],['Liquidity','The ability to trade size in a relevant market with limited cost and delay.'],['Mark-to-market / MTM','Valuation using relevant market prices. MTM is also used informally to mean the resulting market value.'],['Mark-to-model','Valuation using a model, contractual terms and inputs. It can use observable or unobservable inputs.'],['Model risk','Uncertainty from model choice, assumptions or limitations. Known code defects require correction and impact assessment.'],['MPU','Market Price Uncertainty: uncertainty around the current market price or input, distinct from future market risk.'],['Netting set','A defined group subject to enforceable netting arrangements. Eligibility depends on legal and methodological conditions.'],['Notional','Contractual reference amount used in calculations. For derivatives it is generally not the fair value.'],['Observability','The extent to which relevant market evidence supports an input used in the valuation.'],['Override','A controlled exception to a default input or decision, with reason, approver and review/expiry.'],['P&L','Profit and loss. Valuation movement is only one contributor; cash flows, trades and other effects matter.'],['Prudent valuation / PruVal','Assessment of valuation uncertainty for prudential purposes, with applicable additional capital deductions.'],['Proxy','A substitute input based on related instruments or markets, requiring a justified mapping and uncertainty assessment.'],['Repricing','Running the valuation again with a specified alternative input set or model configuration.'],['RWA','Risk-weighted assets, the denominator in a CET1 capital ratio. It is not total notional or total accounting assets.'],['Sensitivity','How much value changes when an input moves by a stated amount, under specified assumptions.'],['Significance','Importance of an input to the overall measurement; not merely whether the input appears in the model.'],['Stale price','An observation too old to support the current measurement under the relevant policy and market conditions.'],['Tolerance','An investigation or escalation threshold; not proof that values within it are correct.'],['Valuation exposure','Under the EU RTS, the amount of a valuation position sensitive to movement in a valuation input. Distinguish it from the valuation position itself (an instrument or portfolio).'],['Vega','Sensitivity to volatility, often per volatility percentage point. Check whether the system uses points or decimal units.'],['Volatility surface','Implied volatilities across strikes and maturities, used in option valuation.']
];
const QUICK = [
['Bond clean value','Signed face × clean price / 100','Per-100 quotation; no FX conversion.'],['Bond dirty value','Clean value + signed accrued interest','Accrued interest must match position and date.'],['Direct IPV difference','Independent value − FO value','A signed diagnostic, not automatic posting authority.'],['First-order input impact','Signed sensitivity × input change','Units aligned; nonlinear effects omitted.'],['Present value','Cash flow / (1 + annual rate)^years','Single cash flow, annual compounding only.'],['Accounting bridge','Raw value + signed accounting adjustments','Teaching labs use positive deduction magnitudes for long assets.'],['Schematic incremental uncertainty','max(0, gross requirement − eligible same-source booked deduction)','Before applicable aggregation; long-asset teaching bridge.'],['CET1 ratio','CET1 / RWA × 100%','Hold RWA fixed only when explicitly assumed.'],['Reserve movement','Closing deduction − opening deduction','An increase is negative value impact for the long-asset convention.']
];
